Welcome to notebook eight. The previous notebook gave you case classes and enums, which is the way Scala expresses data. This notebook gives you the partner operation. Pattern matching. The way you take that data apart again and react to its shape. If you understand pattern matching well, you will write Scala that is easier to read, easier to maintain, and harder to break when the data shape changes underneath you.

If you have come from a C family language like java or C plus plus, the closest cousin to pattern matching is the switch statement. Scala's match expression is far more capable. It can match on literal values like switch can. But it can also match on types. It can deconstruct case classes and enum cases. It can bind sub values to names. It can apply boolean guards on top of the structural match. And, most importantly, it can be checked for exhaustiveness by the compiler, so you cannot accidentally leave a case unhandled.

Let us start with the shape of a match expression. A match takes a scrutinee, which is the expression you write on the left of the match keyword, and tries each case from top to bottom. The first matching case wins. Its right hand side becomes the value of the whole expression. So a match against a value n, with cases for one, two, three, and an underscore default, evaluates the scrutinee once, walks down the cases until one matches, and returns the right hand side of that case.

Five things to register about match expressions. First, a match is an expression, not a statement. It returns a value, which means you can assign the result to a val, or return it from a method. Same mindset as everything else in Scala. Second, each case line has a pattern on the left of the arrow and a result expression on the right. Third, the overall result type is the least upper bound of all the right hand side types. If every case returns a String, the match returns a String. If cases return mixed types, the result is whatever common supertype Scala finds. Fourth, the underscore wildcard pattern matches anything, and when used as the last case it acts as the default fall through. Fifth, order matters. The first matching case wins. So list more specific patterns before more general ones.

Patterns compose, but they are built from a small fixed alphabet. There are seven patterns you will use daily. The literal pattern, like the number forty two, which matches exactly that value using equals. The wildcard pattern, written as an underscore, which matches anything. The variable pattern, like a lowercase x, which matches anything and binds it to that name. The type pattern, like underscore colon String, which matches if the scrutinee is of that type. The tuple pattern, like a comma b in parentheses, which deconstructs a tuple positionally. The constructor pattern, like User of name comma email comma underscore, which deconstructs a case class or enum case. And the sequence pattern, like h colon colon t, which matches a non empty list by splitting head from tail. We will see each one in context as we go.

Literal and wildcard are the simplest. A literal pattern matches exactly that value, using the equals comparison. A wildcard pattern matches anything and binds nothing. Together they let you write switch like dispatch over a finite set of constants.

The variable pattern is where pattern matching starts to feel different. A lowercase identifier in pattern position matches anything and binds the matched value to that name for the right hand side of the case. So writing case n if n is less than zero, arrow, the string negative followed by n, binds n to the value and lets you use it inside the body. This is the difference between a wildcard, which matches and forgets, and a variable pattern, which matches and remembers.

Two important gotchas about variable patterns. First, a lowercase identifier binds. A case with lowercase n binds the matched value to n. Second, an uppercase identifier is treated as a stable reference to an existing value. It does not bind a fresh variable. So writing case None, where None is the standard library singleton, matches the singleton, not a fresh variable named None. This is why constructor names are written in PascalCase. If you ever do need to match against a local lowercase val, you surround the name in backticks, like case backtick expected backtick. That tells the compiler, treat this as a reference, not as a binding.

Type patterns are written as underscore colon T or name colon T. A type pattern matches if the scrutinee is an instance of type T, and it binds the value at that type so you can use it as a T on the right hand side. Type patterns are the safe replacement for the instance of plus cast idiom you see in java. The runtime check and the static cast happen together. The compiler enforces that you use the bound name at the right type. There is one important caveat, though. On the java virtual machine, generic type parameters are erased at runtime. A List of Int and a List of String look identical to the runtime. So a type pattern like List of Int actually checks only that the value is a List. The compiler will warn you about this. The fix is to write List of underscore for any list, and if you need to know the element type, design your code so the static types carry that information through. Notebook ten on generics will revisit erasure.

Tuple patterns deconstruct a tuple positionally. Writing case open paren x comma y close paren binds the two components to x and y. You can combine tuple patterns with guards to refine further. For example, you can decide whether a two dimensional point is on the main diagonal by writing case open paren x comma y close paren if x equals y. The structural match peels off the tuple. The guard refines on the relationship between x and y.

Constructor patterns are where pattern matching becomes the centerpiece of Scala. A constructor pattern matches a case class or an enum case and binds each field to a name. Take the Shape enum from the previous notebook. To compute an area, you match on the shape. Case Shape dot Circle of r, arrow, pi times r squared. Case Shape dot Rectangle of w comma h, arrow, w times h. Case Shape dot Triangle of b comma h, arrow, one half times b times h. Each case both checks which alternative you have and binds the inner fields for use in the body.

Under the hood, this works because every case class generates an unapply method on its companion object. Pattern matching calls unapply, gets the fields back as a tuple, and binds them to the names you wrote. This same mechanism is what lets you write your own custom extractors, but in practice you almost never need to. Case classes and enums cover the common case completely.

Patterns also nest. The fields you bind can themselves be patterns. So you can match an Order case class on three different shapes. The first matches when the customer field is the literal string alice. The second matches when the total field is exactly zero. The third catches anything else with three variable bindings. The compiler tries them top to bottom, and the first match wins.

Guards add a boolean refinement on top of a pattern. You append the keyword if and a boolean expression to a case. The case fires only if both the pattern matches and the guard is true. So a classify method on Int can have a case for zero, a case for any x where x is less than zero, a case for any x where x is less than ten, and a case for everything else. Guards are useful when a condition is not expressible as a pure structural pattern, like comparisons, multi field constraints, or relationships involving external state. Do not over use them, though. If you can refactor the data so the case naturally distinguishes itself, the resulting code is usually clearer.

Alternatives let you share a right hand side across several patterns using the vertical bar. So case lowercase a, vertical bar, lowercase e, vertical bar, lowercase i, vertical bar, lowercase o, vertical bar, lowercase u, arrow, true, recognises any vowel character. There is one caveat though. Alternatives cannot bind variables, because the names would have to agree across alternatives. They only check shape. If you need to bind, use separate cases or a guard.

Sequence patterns are how you work with Lists in idiomatic Scala. There are two shapes you will see constantly. The first is h colon colon t, which matches a non empty list and binds the head element to h and the tail to t. The second is Nil, which matches the empty list. You can also write List of a comma b comma c to match a list of exactly three elements. Combining these gives you the standard recursive pattern. Case Nil arrow empty. Case head colon colon Nil arrow only one element. Case head colon colon rest arrow head plus a recursive call on rest. This is the textbook way to write recursive functions on lists, and you will write many such functions when you start consuming streams of data in Spark.

The at sign binding is a small but powerful trick. Sometimes you want to deconstruct a value and also keep the whole matched value under a name. You write, name at sign pattern. For example, in a tree enum with a Leaf case and a Node case, you can write case full at sign Tree dot Node of underscore comma Leaf comma Leaf. The inner pattern checks that this is a Node with two Leaf children. The outer binding called full refers to the entire Node value. Without the at sign, you would have to repeat the whole node literal in the body. With it, you get to deconstruct and keep the whole at once.

Now we arrive at the most important part of pattern matching. Exhaustiveness checking. This is where pattern matching becomes a design tool, not just a syntax convenience. When the scrutinee's type is a sealed hierarchy, which means every alternative is known to the compiler, the compiler can enumerate the alternatives. Every enum is sealed by construction. Every sealed trait with a fixed list of case class subtypes is also sealed. If your match does not cover every alternative, the compiler emits a warning that the match may not be exhaustive. Treat that warning as an error. It is telling you that there is a real input shape your code silently does not handle.

Now imagine you go and add a new case to an existing enum. Say you add a Cancelled case to PaymentResult. Every match expression in your codebase that did not include the Cancelled case lights up with the same warning. The compiler hands you the worklist. You walk through it. You decide for each match how to handle Cancelled. Some sites may genuinely need the new case ignored, in which case you add a wildcard. Others need real logic. Either way, the compiler told you exactly where to think. This is what makes algebraic data types plus pattern matching such a powerful pairing. The type system becomes the change impact analysis tool.

Pattern matching is not only available inside match expressions. The same machinery works in two other places. First, on the left side of a val binding. You can write val open paren name comma age close paren equals a tuple, and Scala destructures the tuple into the two names. You can also write val User of name comma underscore comma age equals a User value, and Scala uses the unapply method on User to destructure the case class at the binding site. Second, inside a for comprehension. You can write for, open paren name comma age, taking values from the ages Map, if age is at least thirty, do, then print a line. The destructuring of the key value pair happens with the same pattern shape. Both of these forms quietly rely on the same unapply machinery as match expressions. Once you internalise patterns, the same shapes work everywhere a value is bound.

Let us close with a small but realistic example. Classify trading events. You have an Event enum with three cases. Trade, with a symbol, a price, and a quantity. Quote, with a symbol, a bid, and an ask. And Heartbeat, with a timestamp. You write an alert method that matches against an Event. If the event is a Trade with a quantity greater than ten thousand, you flag a large trade. Otherwise if it is a Trade, you describe it. If it is a Quote with a spread greater than one dollar, you flag a wide spread. Otherwise if it is a Quote, you describe it. And if it is a Heartbeat, you simply emit the word heartbeat.

Three things are working together in that example. Constructor patterns deconstruct each event case. Guards refine on quantity and on spread width. Exhaustiveness checking ensures you handle every case. Try deleting the Heartbeat case from the match, and the compiler warns you immediately. Add a new Cancel case to the Event enum, and the alert method lights up as non exhaustive. The type system is pointing you exactly where to think.

Notebook nine puts pattern matching to immediate work on Option, Try, and Either. The three standard library types that let you handle absence, failure, and error union without resorting to null or thrown exceptions. The Some x or None shape from this notebook is exactly how you will deconstruct an Option.
